#Adding whitespaces to strings with tabs or newlines.

#Whitespaces are refered as "nonprinting character", basically spaces and tabs and end of line symbols (binary numbers)
#You can use whitespaces to your advantage, where you can make your code more easy to read.

#Here is the difference between adding the \t (tab) to an output.

print("Python") #Whithout \t

print("\tPython") #With \t

#Now here is an output with \n (newline)

print("Languages:\nPython\nC\nJavaScript")

#You can combine newlines and tabs when coding.

print("Languages:\n\tPython\n\tC\n\tJavaScript")

#Stripping whitespaces

#Whitespaces can confuse programs, python will detect the whitespace and it will see it as another.

#This can be hard when trying to compare strings, e.g checking usernames
#Python does offer a way to remove whitespaces using the rstrip() method.

favorite_language = "Python " #Add a whitespace here

favorite_language.rstrip() #Use rstrip() method and it will remove the whitespace

print(favorite_language) #Now the output is free from whitespaces,

#Now what happens if there is a whitespace on the left side?

favorite_language = " Python"

favorite_language.rstrip()


#As you can see it did nothing, that is because it turns out that rstrip() only removes the whitespaces from the right side.
#For us to remove whitespace from the left side, we need to use lstrip() method.

favorite_language.lstrip()

print(favorite_language)


#If you ever encounter having whitespaces on both sides, you can use the strip() method.

favorite_language = " Python "

favorite_language.strip()


#Removing whitespaces helps you become more familar with manipulating strings and especially when you need to clean up your code.

#Syntax error

#Syntax error is when python doesn't recognize a specific code in your program and therefore you will face an error.

#A commonn example is when you use an apostrophe wrong and python sees an error, here is an example:

